      <!-- <ul class="pq-notes">
        <li><code>@parkquest/types</code> is a shared domain-types package (parks, visits, social, moderation, badges) both apps import as the API contract.</li>
        <li>That package also ships shared SVG glyph geometry for the passport stamps (<code>parkGlyphs.ts</code>) &mdash; web's <code>Stamp</code> component and iOS's <code>ParkStamp</code> render pixel-identical stamp art from the same <code>glyphTransform()</code> function, not duplicated per platform.</li>
        <li>The web app renders its own MapLibre GL vector map; iOS uses react-native-maps with <code>PROVIDER_DEFAULT</code>, which resolves to Apple MapKit rather than Google Maps.</li>
      </ul> -->

    </section>

    <!-- Friend-suggestions algorithm gets its own section rather than a row
         in the API explorer — it's the one piece of real backend logic on
         the page (not just CRUD over a table), and worth spelling out. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-algo-section">
      <h3 class="pq-engineering-subheading">How friend suggestions get ranked</h3>

      <p class="pq-algo-intro">A hybrid graph and behavioral recommendation algorithm suggested potential friend pairings by combining a social-graph traversal (friends-of-friends) and a domain-specific signal (mutually visited parks), weighting the two to produce a score. A fallback ensures that even new users always have suggestions.</p>

      <div class="pq-algo-layout">
        <div class="pq-algo-text">
          <ol class="pq-algo-steps">
            {suggestionSteps.map((step) => (
              <li class="pq-algo-step">
                <span class="pq-algo-step-num">{step.n}</span>
                <div class="pq-algo-step-body">
                  <h4 class="pq-algo-step-title">{step.title}</h4>
                  <p class="pq-algo-step-desc">{step.desc}</p>
                  {step.formula && <code class="pq-algo-formula">{step.formula}</code>}
                </div>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>

        <div class="pq-algo-visual" data-stage="all" aria-hidden="true">
          <svg class="pq-sugg-graph" viewBox={`0 0 ${SUGG_SIZE} ${SUGG_SIZE}`}>
            <!-- Signal A, drawn: a candidate isn't just linked to you, some
                 are also linked to a friend directly — the actual
                 friends-of-friends relationship "mutual" counts. Faint and
                 behind everything else on purpose; the you-to-everyone
                 spokes are still the primary structure. -->
            <g class="pq-sugg-fof-edges">
              {suggFoFEdges.map((e) => (
                <line class="pq-sugg-fof-edge" x1={e.x1} y1={e.y1} x2={e.x2} y2={e.y2} />
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-edges">
              {suggPeople.map((p, i) => (
                <line
                  class="pq-sugg-edge"
                  data-friend={p.isFriend ? "true" : undefined}
                  x1={suggEdges[i].x1}
                  y1={suggEdges[i].y1}
                  x2={suggEdges[i].x2}
                  y2={suggEdges[i].y2}
                />
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-people">
              {suggPeople.map((p) => (
                <g
                  class="pq-sugg-node"
                  data-id={p.id}
                  data-friend={p.isFriend ? "true" : undefined}
                  data-top={p.id === suggTopCandidateId ? "true" : undefined}
                  transform={`translate(${p.x} ${p.y})`}
                >
                  <circle class="pq-sugg-dot" r={SUGG_NODE_R} />
                  <text class="pq-sugg-initials" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">{p.initials}</text>
                  {!p.isFriend && (
                    <text class="pq-sugg-score" y={-SUGG_NODE_R - 8} text-anchor="middle">{p.score}</text>
                  )}
                </g>
              ))}
            </g>
            <g class="pq-sugg-you" transform={`translate(${SUGG_CENTER} ${SUGG_CENTER})`}>
              <circle r={SUGG_YOU_R} />
              <text y="5" text-anchor="middle">You</text>
            </g>
          </svg>

          <!-- Same graph, tabular form — the pairing a graph-theory text
               uses (visual + adjacency/attribute table), not either alone.
               Status reveals (friends struck through) at "exclude"; the
               real two signals the algorithm scores on — mutual (friends-
               of-friends) and shared (co-visited parks) — reveal one at a
               time at "fof" and "parks", then the formula's result at
               "score"; at "rank" the rows themselves reorder by score, in
               place, instead of handing off to a separate list. -->
          <table class="pq-sugg-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Person</th><th>Status</th><th>FoF</th><th>Parks</th><th class="pq-sugg-th-score">Score</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {suggPeople.map((p) => (
                <tr data-id={p.id} data-friend={p.isFriend ? "true" : undefined} data-top={p.id === suggTopCandidateId ? "true" : undefined} data-score={p.isFriend ? -1 : p.score}>
                  <td class="pq-sugg-row-person">
                    <span class="pq-sugg-row-rank">{p.isFriend ? "" : suggRankById.get(p.id)}</span>
                    <span class="pq-sugg-row-avatar">{p.initials}</span>
                    <span class="pq-sugg-row-name">{p.name}</span>
                  </td>
                  <td class="pq-sugg-row-status">{p.isFriend ? "Friend" : "Candidate"}</td>
                  <td class="pq-sugg-row-fof">{p.isFriend ? "—" : p.mutual}</td>
                  <td class="pq-sugg-row-parks">{p.isFriend ? "—" : p.shared}</td>
                  <td class="pq-sugg-row-score">{p.isFriend ? "—" : p.score}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <!-- Same Apple-style replay affordance as the phone mockup
               (pq-replay): appears once the cycle settles on "rank" and
               dwells there, restarting the whole thing on click instead of
               waiting out the rest of the auto-replay timer. -->
          <button type="button" id="pq-sugg-replay" class="pq-sugg-replay" aria-label="Replay">
            <svg class="pq-replay-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-3-6.7" />
              <path d="M21 3v6h-6" />
            </svg>
            Replay
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Offline mode gets its own section for the same reason the friend-
         suggestions algorithm does: it's a real design tradeoff driven by a
         concrete usage constraint, not just another CRUD surface. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-offline-section">
      <h3 class="pq-engineering-subheading">Offline mode</h3>
      <p class="pq-offline-intro">National parks are exactly where cell coverage is worst — deep canyons, mountain terrain, no towers for miles. A user who already used the app to plan a hike loses the whole point of it if they're standing at a trailhead with no bars and the app needs a live connection to show anything. Every decision below optimizes for one thing: works with zero connectivity, once downloaded.</p>

      <div class="pq-offline-tiers">
        <article class="pq-offline-tier">
          <div class="pq-offline-tier-body">
            <h4 class="pq-tier-title">Tier 1 &mdash; automatic</h4>
            <p>Lightweight, silent, automatic. Every ordinary visit to the Parks or Map tab quietly re-saves the base park list &mdash; pins, name, About text, cover photo &mdash; to on-device storage. No user action, just a side effect of normal browsing.</p>
            <p class="pq-offline-tier-note">Refreshes any time the network is up and that tab loads &mdash; and when it isn't, the screen falls through to this cache and shows an offline banner instead of an error.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="pq-offline-shot pq-offline-shot-banner">
            <img src="/showcase/parkquest/web/offline-banner.jpg" alt="Top of the ParkQuest parks screen with the offline banner showing, still listing cached parks" loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
        </article>

        <article class="pq-offline-tier">
          <div class="pq-offline-tier-body">
            <h4 class="pq-tier-title">Tier 2 &mdash; explicit</h4>
            <p>Heavy, deliberate, user-triggered. A "Download for offline" button bulk-fetches full park detail for every park in one call &mdash; galleries, hours, fees, activities, topics, directions &mdash; and prefetches every image byte to disk. Caching a URL string isn't enough; nothing has actually fetched those bytes until something tries to render that exact URL, which doesn't happen offline.</p>
            <p class="pq-offline-tier-note">Only updates on an explicit tap &mdash; it's an expensive bulk operation &mdash; and the cache's age surfaces right there ("Last updated 3 days ago"), so stale data reads as stale, not as live.</p>
          </div>
          <figure class="pq-offline-shot pq-offline-shot-settings">
            <img src="/showcase/parkquest/web/offline-settings.jpg" alt="ParkQuest settings screen with the last-saved timestamp and the Download for offline button" loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="pq-offline-detail">
        <div class="pq-offline-callout">
          <h4>Why two separate caches, not one</h4>
          <p>If the two tiers shared a cache, the silent background refresh &mdash; which only ever has the lightweight fields &mdash; would overwrite a user's deliberately-downloaded rich data with a partial blob the next time they opened the tab. Splitting the keys was a direct fix for that clobbering bug.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pq-offline-callout">
          <h4>Never cache an empty result over a good one</h4>
          <p>The bulk park-detail endpoint silently returns nothing if the upstream park-service key is missing or the request fails. The download handler checks for that and throws instead of quietly replacing a full cache with an empty one.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="pq-offline-fallback">Network status wraps the OS-level connectivity check, treating the ambiguous state at startup as online so the app doesn't flash a false offline banner before the first real signal arrives. Screens try the live fetch first &mdash; on failure, or when auth hasn't finished loading yet (which looks identical to offline at startup), they fall through to the cached data and show an offline banner instead of an error or blank screen. The main feed gets the same treatment at a smaller scale &mdash; the last successfully-loaded page stays scrollable with no connection.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Admin dashboard — split into its own section, not a subsection of
         Built With: it's a genuinely separate surface (private, admin-only,
         not linked anywhere else on this page) and deserved its own
         heading instead of living under an h3. -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-admin-section">
      <h3 class="pq-engineering-subheading">Behind the curtain</h3>
      <p class="pq-routes-sub">An admin-side dashboard allows for moderating the platform's content, creating new badges and challenges, and analyzing global trends and performance. Multiple tabs are available for different aspects of the admin experience.</p>

      <div class="pq-admin-layout">
        <div class="pq-admin-text">
          <div class="pq-admin-tool-list">
            {adminTools.map((tool) => (
              <div class="pq-admin-tool-row">
                <div class="pq-admin-tool-head">
                  <span class="pq-admin-tool-icon" set:html={`<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">${ROUTE_ICONS[tool.icon]}</svg>`} />
                  <span class="pq-admin-tool-label">{tool.label}</span>
                </div>
                <span class="pq-admin-tool-desc">{tool.desc}</span>
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="pq-laptop-stage">
          <div class="pq-laptop">
            <div class="pq-laptop-screen-hole">
              <img src="/showcase/parkquest/web/admin-dashboard.jpg" alt="ParkQuest admin dashboard" loading="lazy" />
            </div>
            <div class="pq-laptop-outline" set:html={macbookOutline} />
            <svg class="pq-laptop-chrome" viewBox="0 0 3860 2540" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <line class="pq-hinge-line" x1="364" y1="2371" x2="3494" y2="2371"></line>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="pq-laptop-caption">Figures shown are illustrative.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="pq-admin-other">Why it exists: many aspects of the app are easier to manage from a web dashboard than from within the app. Per Apple's App Store Review Guidelines 1.2 (user-generated content), this dashboard serves as the moderation surface for user-generated content, and additionally allows for customizing the user experience and analyzing trends for optimal marketing strategy.</p>

      <div class="pq-admin-feature">
        <h4>Reconciling Apple's App Store Connect reporting</h4>
        <p>A daily Vercel Cron job merges Apple's two separate, laggy reporting pipelines into one metrics table.</p>

        <div class="pq-recon-diagram">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 640 300" class="pq-recon-diagram-svg" aria-hidden="true">
            <g fill="none" stroke="var(--pq-gold)" stroke-width="1.4" opacity="0.7">
              <line x1="320" y1="78" x2="170" y2="120" marker-end="url(#pq-diagram-arrow)" />
              <line x1="320" y1="78" x2="470" y2="120" marker-end="url(#pq-diagram-arrow)" />
              <line x1="170" y1="190" x2="320" y2="224" marker-end="url(#pq-diagram-arrow)" />
              <line x1="470" y1="190" x2="320" y2="224" marker-end="url(#pq-diagram-arrow)" />
            </g>

            <g class="pq-recon-node-ours">
              <rect x="220" y="18" width="200" height="60" rx="10" />
            </g>
            <g class="pq-recon-node-service">
              <rect x="30" y="120" width="280" height="70" rx="10" />
              <rect x="330" y="120" width="280" height="70" rx="10" />
            </g>
            <g class="pq-recon-node-ours">
              <rect x="190" y="224" width="260" height="60" rx="10" />
            </g>

            <g text-anchor="middle">
              <text x="320" y="44" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="var(--pq-cream)">Vercel Cron</text>
              <text x="320" y="62" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--pq-mute)">Runs daily</text>

              <text x="170" y="150" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="var(--pq-cream)">Sales Report API</text>
              <text x="170" y="168" font-size="11" fill="var(--pq-mute)">Apple App Store Connect</text>

              <text x="470" y="150" font-size="14" font-weight="700" fill="var(--pq-cream)">Analytics Report API</text>
              <text x="470" y="168" font-size="11" fill="var(--pq-mute)">Apple App Store Connect</text>

              <text x="320" y="250" font-size="15" font-weight="700" fill="var(--pq-cream)">Metrics table</text>
              <text x="320" y="268" font-size="11.5" fill="var(--pq-mute)">One row per park &middot; day</text>

              <text x="320" y="102" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--pq-mute)">ES256 JWT &middot; Apple .p8 key</text>
              <text x="320" y="211" font-size="10.5" fill="var(--pq-mute)">idempotent upsert</text>
            </g>
          </svg>
        </div>

        <p class="pq-recon-caption">Auth is hand-rolled: an ES256 JWT signed with Apple's <code>.p8</code> key via Node's <code>crypto</code> directly, no JWT library &mdash; App Store Connect tokens cap at 20 minutes. Both report APIs land in different formats on different lag, and get idempotently upserted into one table so a partial or repeat run can't clobber good data.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
    </div>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="pq-section pq-cta">
